WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.740
The all-star cast of Mamma Mia is back for
more sun sea and ABBA songs!

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.590
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers it's Jan here and
today I'm talking about the very best and

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:13.809
the not-so-great bits of the Mamma Mia sequel
and comparing it to the original movie so

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:16.170
obviously I'll be discussing spoilers.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:20.539
By the way keep watching for details of my
very special Mamma Mia giveaway.

00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:24.369
Because Mamma Mia 2 is both a prequel and
a sequel we get lots of new actors joining

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:26.829
the original cast and they're all really great.

00:00:26.829 --> 00:00:30.300
A particular standout is Lily James who plays
the young Donna.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.691
She really has tons of energy and spirit in
the role, a great voice, really gives her

00:00:34.691 --> 00:00:38.720
all in the dance routines and works brilliantly
with all the new actors playing the younger

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.820
versions of her friends and lovers.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:45.440
Then there's also Jeremy Irvine who plays
the young salmon he is just fantastic in the

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.440
role.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.930
Now when I first heard that he'd been cast
as the young Pierce Brosnan character I wondered

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:54.320
why he hadn't been cast as the young version
of Colin Firth's character Harry instead.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.820
Because he's done that before very well in
a film called The Railway Man.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.721
But as a young Sam in Mamma Mia 2, Irvine
really does do an incredible take on a young

00:01:01.721 --> 00:01:02.721
Pierce Brosnan.

00:01:02.721 --> 00:01:06.719
Hugh Skinner and Josh Dylan are also great
as the young Harry and Bill.

00:01:06.719 --> 00:01:11.850
Also new to the cast is Maria Vacratsis who
plays Sofia who gets some little scene stealing

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:16.140
moments especially a short scene that she
has with the young Sam which I thought was

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.310
just perfect.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:23.200
Also just brilliant in this film is the actress
who plays young Tanya and she's called Jessica

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.369
Keenan Wynn.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:29.689
She absolutely perfectly channels the spirit
of Christine Baranski's Tanya.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:34.329
Again like Jeremy Irving with Pierce Brosnan
she's not doing an impression of Christine

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:38.649
Baranski but she's taking those aspects a
little idiosyncrasies in the way she speaks

00:01:38.649 --> 00:01:42.280
her cadence and things, taking some of them
putting them into the younger role of Tanya

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.979
but also bringing other things apart as well.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:48.649
Young Rosie who's played by Alexa Davies also
brings some nice little moments as

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:51.709
well so you can really look forward to seeing
those two on screen.

00:01:51.709 --> 00:01:56.900
And the moment where young Tanya tells young
Sam that she visually enjoys him is just priceless.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:02.649
As for the returning cast I cannot get enough
of Christine Baranski and Julie Walters as

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:05.450
Donna's friends and fellow dynamos Tanya and
Rosie.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.210
"The beauty comes not only from the exquisite
structure of your bones and your laughter

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:15.930
and your sparkling eyes but from the goodness
that flowers within you like a fountain."

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.684
"Have him washed and brought to my tent."

00:02:18.684 --> 00:02:19.920
"I don't think fountains flower, did they?"

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:20.920
"Mine does."

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.879
And then there's a hilarious way they sing
Angel Eyes to Sophie with Christine Baranski's

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:29.150
Tanya hardly letting Judy Walters' Rosie get
a word in edgeways.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.959
Of course the prequel aspect of this film
means there are obviously fewer scenes with

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:36.930
the original cast which of course is a bit
of a shame because they're all so great.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:41.450
So for example while we do see Colin Firth,
Stellan Skarsgard and Pierce Brosnan sing,

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:45.930
dance and act, we do see them doing less of
that than we did in the first film.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.810
Speaking of the original cast, someone I would
have loved have seen much more of is Meryl

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:50.960
Streep as Donna.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.090
I just adored scenes Streep singing, dancing
and acting with gusto in the first film and

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.819
I really did miss her here.

00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:02.469
Now while we certainly don't get enough Meryl
Streep in the movie we do get the added benefit

00:03:02.469 --> 00:03:07.060
of Cher in a role that was written specifically
for her and which fits her like a glittery

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:08.060
glove.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:12.040
Is it a touch insane that Cher who's only
three years older than Meryl Streep in real

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.849
life is playing Donna's mother?

00:03:13.849 --> 00:03:14.849
Of course it is!

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:16.239
Do I care?

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:17.239
Absolutely not.

00:03:17.239 --> 00:03:22.020
Because at the end of the day we get to hear
Cher sing Fernando complete with a big fireworks

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:25.540
display and we get some crazy moments with
Sophie's grandma.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:31.370
Now when it comes to the music obviously in
the first film we got an absolute ton of Abbas

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:36.930
biggest greatest hits which means that in
this film we get some lesser-known Abba tracks

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:41.459
like When I Kissed The Teacher, Why Did It
Have To Be Me, Kisses Of Fire and Andaunte,

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:44.250
Andante, I've Been Waiting For You and My
Love, My Life.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:48.209
But we do also get some songs that weren't
in the original film that you will - I think

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:49.489
- definitely recognize.

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:52.239
There's The Name Of The Game which is performed
by Lily James.

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:56.640
There's Knowing Me, Knowing You which is again
Lily James and Jeremy Irvine and there's also

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.010
Angel Eyes and Fernando as well and not to
worry because we do still get many of the

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:04.500
hugely popular hits from the first film.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:09.409
So there's Mamma Mia, Dancing Queen, Super
Trouper and Waterloo to name a few.

00:04:09.409 --> 00:04:14.400
For Dancing Queen we get a flotilla of fishermen
along with Stellan Skarsgard and Colin Firth

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:17.880
recreating the Titanic pose as they sailed
towards the island.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.650
And for Waterloo it's in a Parisian restaurant
with a young Harry and Donna and we even get

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.870
an Abba cameo as well with Benny playing the
piano.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:30.500
And Mamma Mia gets a spirited performance
by the Young Dynamos this time when the film

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:35.710
proper ends we get a fantastic version of
Super Trouper with all the main cast joining

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:36.710
in.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:42.310
While Super Trouper was amazing I was a little
bit disappointed that we only got one song

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:47.490
and dance routine in the kind of curtain call
cast party moment in the film this time around

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.930
'cos last time if you remember we got two
songs.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:55.280
We got Dancing Queen and Waterloo so it would
have been fantastic if we just got one more.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:01.740
Now if you stay to the very end of the credits
after everyone's names rolled by we do get

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.039
a little unexpected extra something.

00:05:04.039 --> 00:05:08.860
There's an unexpected post-credits scene which
makes up a little bit for only one extra song

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:09.860
after the film finishes.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:14.470
The post credits scene features comic actor
Omid Djalili as the Greek official who was

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:18.550
checking people's passports before they boarded
the island ferry earlier in the film.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:22.430
If you remember he had quite a few opinions
about what people look like in their passport

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:26.639
photos compared to real life and usually told
them they look better in the photo except

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:30.919
in the case of Colin Firth's Harry who he
said looked great and had aged like a fine

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:31.919
wine.

00:05:31.919 --> 00:05:35.270
If you missed the post-credits scene it basically
shows a young Harry has followed Donna to

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:39.539
Greece where the Greek official tells him
if she rejects you give me a call.

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:44.060
Then he follows up his proposition by singing
a few lines from Take A Chance On Me.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:48.240
Overall then while there are some more melancholy
and wistful moments in this film compared

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.940
to the first there's still a boatload of joyous
effervescent scenes overflowing with great

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:54.460
song and dance routines.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:59.440
And though it's not quite the non-stop charmer
the original was, my my, how could I resist

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:00.440
it?

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:05.039
So yes I did thoroughly enjoy Mamma Mia 2
and will definitely be watching it again hopefully

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:06.039
soon.

00:06:06.039 --> 00:06:09.080
Now how do you think the Mamma Mia sequel
compares to the first film?

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.380
And what are your favorite moments or songs
in the new movie?

